urname>Tomita-Mitchell</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Furth</surname><given-names>EE</given-names></name><name><surname>Morgenthaler</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Thilly</surname><given-names>WG</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Population risk and physiological rate parameters for colon cancer: The union of an explicit model for carcinogenesis with the public health records of the United States</article-title><source>Mutation Research &#x02013; Fundam Molec Mechan Mutagenesis</source><year>2000</year><volume>447</volume><fpage>73</fpage><lpage>116</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10686307</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0027-5107(99)00201-8</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hoem</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>On the statistical theory of analytic graduation</article-title><source>Proceedings of the Sixth Berkeley Symposium on Mathematical Statistics and Probability (Univ California, Berkeley, Calif, 1970/1971), Theory of statistics</source><year>1972</year><volume>I</volume><publisher-name>Berkeley, Calif.: Univ. California Press</publisher-name><fpage>569</fpage><lpage>600</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="B25"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hoem</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The statistical theory of demographic rates. A review of current developments</article-title><source>Scand J Statist</source><year>1976</year><volume>3</volume><fpage>169</fpage><lpage>185</lpage><comment>[With discussion by Niels Keiding, Hannu Kulokari, Bent Natvig, Ole Barndorff-Nielsen, J&#x000f8;rgen Hilden and a reply by the author].</comment></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>